  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Resource Development / Acquire Infrastructure — Domains + DNS Server", "technique": "T1583.001", "what_happened": "At 2026-03-09 02:14:07 CET, internal host 10.108.57.42 issued a DNS A-record query for update-corp.duckdns.org via the corporate resolver 10.108.57.1. duckdns.org is a well-known FREE dynamic-DNS provider — attackers acquire DDNS subdomains because they are free, instantly provisioned, and can be re-pointed to any IP at will, making them ideal cheap, rotatable C2 infrastructure. The 'update-corp' subdomain label is a deliberate corporate-themed lure designed to blend into legitimate-looking traffic. Sysmon Event ID 22 (DnsQuery) on the endpoint and Suricata DNS logging on the resolver path both recorded the lookup. This is the canonical Resource-Development fingerprint: an internal asset reaching out to adversary-acquirable free infrastructure.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event ID 22 with data.win.eventdata.queryName=update-corp.duckdns.org on agent 10.108.57.42, plus a Suricata DNS event with data.dns.rrname=update-corp.duckdns.org and data.dns.rrtype=A. rule.mitre.id T1583.001 (Acquire Infrastructure: Domains) was attached by the Wazuh rule because the queried zone matches the curated free/dynamic-DNS provider list.", "first_seen": "2026-03-09T02:14:07+0100"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Resource Development / Acquire Infrastructure — Botnet-like fan-out across DDNS providers", "technique": "T1584", "what_happened": "Between 02:14:07 and 02:15:02 CET (~55 seconds), the same internal host 10.108.57.42 issued a rapid sequential burst of DNS A-record queries for EIGHT distinct hostnames spanning SEVEN different free/dynamic-DNS providers: update-corp.duckdns.org, secure-mail.no-ip.com, portal-corp.dyndns.org, helper-srv.zapto.org, corp-vpn.changeip.com, helper.publicvm.com, update.servehttp.com, and corp.hopto.org. Querying a fan-out of multiple DDNS providers in one tight burst is a beacon-resolution / C2-failover pattern: malware resolves a list of fallback C2 domains so that if one provider sinkholes or takes down a domain, the implant rotates to the next. The corporate-themed labels (update-corp, secure-mail, portal-corp, corp-vpn) across all eight are a consistent lure naming convention pointing to a single operator.", "chain_reason": "Eight Sysmon Event ID 22 records on agent 10.108.57.42 within a 55-second window, each data.win.eventdata.queryName matching a distinct entry on the curated free/dynamic-DNS provider list, plus matching Suricata data.dns.rrname events with data.dns.rrtype=A. rule.mitre.id T1584 (Compromise Infrastructure) attached by the correlation rule on the multi-provider fan-out pattern from a single internal source.", "first_seen": "2026-03-09T02:14:12+0100"}
